Supreme People's Court

Opinion on Regulating the Operation Mechanism of the Collegial Panel

Fa Fa [2022] No. 31

To comprehensively and accurately implement the judicial accountability system, regulate the operation mechanism of collegial panels, and clarify the duties of collegial panels, this Opinion is formulated in accordance with the Organic Law of the People's Courts of the People's Republic of China, the Judges Law of the People's Republic of China, the Criminal Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, the Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, the Administrative Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, and other relevant laws and judicial interpretations, and in light of the actual work of the people's courts.

1. The collegial panel is the basic adjudicative body of the people's court. All members of the collegial panel participate equally in adjudicative activities such as reviewing case files, conducting trials, deliberating, and issuing judgments. They independently express opinions on issues such as the admissibility of evidence, fact-finding, application of law, procedural matters, and the outcome of the case, and bear corresponding responsibility therefor.

2. A collegial panel may be formed by designation or random selection. Where, due to the need for specialized adjudication or the division of cases into simple and complex ones, the members of a collegial panel are relatively fixed, they shall rotate periodically. For cases that fall within the "four types of cases" or are supervised and managed by reference to the "four types of cases," the president of the court or the division chief may designate the members of the collegial panel within their authority. Where a collegial panel is formed by designation, a complete record shall be left on the case-handling platform, or a written record shall be created and placed on file for future reference.

The presiding judge of the collegial panel shall be designated by the president of the court or the division chief. Where the president of the court or the division chief participates in the collegial panel, the president or division chief shall serve as the presiding judge.

After the members of the collegial panel are determined, if it is truly necessary to adjust the members due to recusal, job transfer, health reasons, integrity risks, or other grounds, the adjustment shall be decided by the president of the court or the division chief within their authority. The result of the adjustment shall be promptly notified to the parties, and the reason shall be noted on the case-handling platform, or a written record shall be created and placed on file for future reference.

For cases where the law or judicial interpretation requires "forming a new collegial panel," neither the original members of the collegial panel nor the judicial assistants shall participate in handling the case.

3. When a collegial panel hears a case, in addition to the duties jointly borne by the members of the collegial panel, the presiding judge shall also perform the following duties:

(1) Determining the case handling plan and trial outline, coordinating the division of work among the collegial panel members during trial, and guiding the collegial panel members or judicial assistants in making other necessary trial preparations;

(2) Presiding over and directing the trial activities;

(3) Presiding over the deliberation of the collegial panel;

(4) Proposing, in accordance with relevant provisions and procedures, to submit cases where there are significant disagreements among the collegial panel members to the professional judges' meeting for discussion, or to the adjudication committee for discussion and decision;

(5) Exercising other adjudicative powers in accordance with the law.

When the presiding judge handles a case as the responsible judge, the presiding judge shall also perform the duties of the judge handling the case.

4. When a collegial panel hears a case, the judge handling the case shall perform the following duties:

(1) Presiding over or guiding judicial assistants in preparing for pre-trial conferences, pre-trial mediation, discovery, and other pre-trial preparations, as well as other judicial assistance work;

(2) Submitting matters such as objections to jurisdiction raised by the parties, and applications for preservation measures, judicial appraisal, witness testimony, and exclusion of illegally obtained evidence, for deliberation by the collegial panel;

(3) Comprehensively reviewing the evidence involved in the case and presenting a review opinion;

(4) Drafting the case handling plan and trial outline, and preparing notes on the case file review as needed for the case;

(5) Assisting the presiding judge in carrying out trial activities;

(6) Participating in the deliberation of the case and presenting a preliminary opinion on the disposition;

(7) Drafting or guiding judicial assistants in drafting the trial report, similar case search report, etc., as needed for the case;

(8) Drafting adjudicative documents, etc., based on the deliberation opinion of the collegial panel or the decision of the adjudication committee;

(9) Exercising other adjudicative powers in accordance with the law.

5. When a collegial panel hears a case, the other members of the collegial panel shall jointly participate in adjudicative activities such as reviewing case files, conducting trials, and deliberating, and shall complete corresponding adjudicative work as arranged by the presiding judge.

6. The collegial panel shall conduct deliberation promptly after the trial. Where collegial panel members are genuinely unable to conduct in-person deliberation at the same location due to objective reasons, they may conduct deliberation online through the case-handling platform of the people's court; however, they may not participate in deliberation by submitting written opinions or by entrusting others to participate. The deliberation process of the collegial panel shall not be open to personnel who have not directly participated in the hearing of the case.

When the collegial panel deliberates a case, the judge handling the case shall first present opinions on fact-finding, admissibility of evidence, and application of law, and the other collegial panel members shall present opinions in turn. The presiding judge shall summarize the conclusive opinions of the collegial panel based on the deliberation.

When presiding over deliberation, the presiding judge has equal rights with the other members of the collegial panel. When deliberating, collegial panel members shall fully state their opinions and exercise their voting rights independently; they may not refuse to state opinions. Where they agree with another person's opinion, they shall provide factual and legal grounds and demonstrate the reasoning.

The voting of collegial panel members on the outcome of deliberation shall be conducted orally. The deliberation process shall be fully recorded in writing in the deliberation record, which shall be prepared by a judicial assistant and signed by the participants in the deliberation and the preparer. The deliberation record constitutes a trial secret and shall not be disclosed outside unless under statutory procedures and conditions.

7. If there is a difference of opinion during deliberation of the collegial panel, the decision shall be made in accordance with the majority opinion, but the minority opinion shall be recorded in the record.

The collegial panel may reconsider a case based on the circumstances of the case or supervision opinions raised by the president of the court or the division chief. If the collegial panel is unable to reach a majority opinion, the presiding judge shall, in accordance with relevant provisions and procedures, suggest that the president of the court or the division chief submit the case to the professional judges' meeting for discussion, or the president of the court shall submit the case to the adjudication committee for discussion and decision. Opinions formed by the professional judges' meeting during discussion shall serve as reference for the collegial panel's reconsideration; the collegial panel shall implement decisions of the adjudication committee.

8. If the collegial panel discovers that the case being heard falls within the "four types of cases" or that it is necessary to supervise and manage by reference to the "four types of cases," it shall promptly report to the president of the court or the division chief in accordance with relevant provisions.

For cases that fall within the "four types of cases" or are supervised and managed by reference to the "four types of cases," the president of the court or the division chief may, within their authority, require the collegial panel to report on the progress of the case hearing and the outcome of deliberation, raise opinions on relevant issues involved in the case hearing, and, as circumstances warrant, suggest that the collegial panel reconsider. If the president of the court or the division chief disagrees with the trial process or the outcome of deliberation or reconsideration, they may decide to submit the case to the professional judges' meeting for discussion, or submit the case to the adjudication committee for discussion and decision in accordance with procedures; however, they may not directly change the opinion of the collegial panel. The supervision and management activities of the president of the court or the division chief shall be fully documented on the case-handling platform, or a written record shall be created and placed on file for future reference.

9. Adjudicative documents formed as a result of a case heard by a collegial panel shall be signed by the members of the collegial panel, who shall bear joint responsibility. Before signing, other members of the collegial panel may propose revision opinions on the adjudicative document and provide feedback to the judge handling the case.

10. This Opinion applies where a collegial panel composed of judges hears a case. The operation mechanism for collegial panels composed of judges and people's assessors in accordance with the law shall be separately provided. Matters that require deliberation or review by a collegial panel during the handling of enforcement cases shall be handled by reference to this Opinion.

11. This Opinion shall take effect as of November 1, 2022. Where previous provisions are inconsistent with this Opinion, this Opinion shall prevail.

October 26, 2022
